gh into  headline cpi we do however see core cbi  pressures continue to build this is  something we talked about last on the  show with the new acceleration to an  all-time high median cpi on a  three-month annualized basis sticky cpi  accelerated to 40 uh year high on  in terms of on a theoreticalized basis  as well so we are seeing a kind of a  blue push higher in core inflation but  it might be overwhelmed by declining  food and energy prices as it relates to  headline  as we begin to look at some of those  inflation measurements uh they continue  to talk about 9.1 percent uh  in a couple weeks ago you and i were  talking and you were talking about the  acceleration of kind of the three-month  uh annualized inflation do you think  that that plays into kind of their  continued aggressiveness is even though  they only talk about europe  this is a problem right like we all know  that inflation should eventually come  down on a year over your rate of change  basis just due to base effects uh we  don't need to unpack base effects here  that's probably not the right audience  but  but the problem with you know kind of  anchoring on base effects uh to get  inflation down which i think a lot of  investors are making this mistake which  is if you only rely on base effects to  get inflation down you're going to wind  up with a structurally higher kind of a  base rate based level of inflation by  the end of that process and so it's my  belief and and i think you know just  based on the kind of the tea leaves we  continue to hear out of fed over out of  fed heads and obviously out of power  yesterday is that the annualized the  momentum the sequential change in  inflation is really what's driving the  boat from a policy standpoint and the  most recent data points we're getting  you know if you look at on a three-month  annualized rate of change basis like  pretty much everything in the entire  inflation basket is accelerating to the  upside with the exception of energy so i 